  <h2>Why take the HMI out of the runtime</h2>
  <p>Operators get a screen without a download to the PLC, and the PLC gets a second set of eyes that does not depend on anything else on the node. A screen in a browser tab on a phone or a wall is a screen your PLC does not have to render.</p>

  <h2>How it reads your PLC</h2>
  <p>CODESYS documents one way for a third party to read a controller's variables, and it is the runtime's own OPC UA server. You publish what you want seen with a Symbol Configuration, and HotLoop reads the variables by the names the programmer gave them, like <code>Application.GVL_Line.rTemp</code>. Arrays and structures are walked into one point per field, and a restart or a download reconnects with no hands on it.</p>

  <h2>What you get</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>What it does for you</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Screens</td><td>A default interface for a panel PC, a phone, or a wall, drawn from one profile file.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Alarms</td><td>Alarms on your PLC variables, with the history kept next to them.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Your existing screen</td><td>Import a CODESYS visualization export and it opens on the same variables.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Hands off the PLC</td><td>Read-only by default, nothing in the runtime, and a write gate if you ever arm a tag.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Keeps its screens through a lost link</td><td>The pods tolerate an unreachable node with no time limit, and the screens come back when it does.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Pair it with the Transmitter</h2>
  <p>If the PLC should push values out instead of waiting to be read, <a href="/transmitter/">HotLoop Transmitter</a> is a CODESYS library that publishes them as OPC UA PubSub over UDP, written in Structured Text.</p>
